import { p, family } from "./seedHelpers";

// EVERYDAY & LONG-TAIL DEPARTMENTS
// ===============================
// Grocery · Baby & Kids · Books & Stationery · Automotive · Health & Wellness
// · Pet Supplies · Tools & Home Improvement · Toys & Games · Sports & Fitness
//
// This is the file that decides whether the catalogue architecture is real. A
// ₹45 pen and a ₹1.4 lakh treadmill flow through exactly the same Product →
// Listing → Offer → Observation graph, the same specification registry and the
// same pricing engine. Nothing here is special-cased.
//
// Two properties are deliberate:
//
//  1. COMMODITY PRICE CLUSTERING. Biscuits, LED bulbs, notebooks and spices sit
//     within a few rupees of each other. These are the categories where a
//     seller has almost no pricing freedom, and the engine should return a very
//     narrow band with high confidence — the opposite of the segmented markets
//     in the fashion file.
//
//  2. GENUINELY THIN MARKETS. Treadmills, glucometers, strollers and cricket
//     bats have only two to four products each. That is not an omission: a real
//     marketplace has long-tail categories where five true competitors do not
//     exist, and the recommendation engine has to say so rather than pad the
//     set. These rows are the test fixtures for the refusal path.

